course: dictionary
lesson: 4245
title: Granola: a food and a snack
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are foods, and a food word can mean more than one thing. Granola is a word you will see on cereal boxes and snack wrappers. «She finishes her granola bar on the trail.» The word bar tells you the granola has been made into a snack you can hold in your hand. The word trail tells you she is out walking, probably needing energy.
explain: On its own, granola means the loose mix of baked oats, nuts, seeds and dried fruit, often sweetened with honey. Look at «a bowl of granola with yogurt». You eat it at breakfast, with milk or yogurt. You cannot count it like apples, so you say some granola, not two granolas. But a granola bar is one bar, so you can count it.
explain: Context clues help you find the meaning. A bowl, milk and yogurt point to breakfast. A trail and a bar point to a snack on a walk. When you meet granola, ask yourself: what would I eat this with, and when would I eat it?
fact: Granola is a breakfast food of baked oats, nuts, seeds and dried fruit.
fact: A granola bar is a snack bar made from these ingredients.
fact: Granola is uncountable as a food, so you say some granola, not a granola.
fact: Granola is common for breakfast and snacks in the USA, Canada and Australia.
fact: In the UK, similar food is often called «crunchy muesli» or «granola».
exercise: «She sprinkled granola on her yogurt.» What is granola? | A cold drink made from milk | *A breakfast food of baked oats, nuts, seeds and dried fruit | A type of sports shoe for running
exercise: Which of these is one snack you can hold in your hand? | A bowl of granola | Some granola with milk | *A granola bar
exercise: The label says «high in sugar». What should you do? | Eat the whole packet because it is breakfast | Throw the granola away at once | *Read the label and check how much sugar it has | Ignore labels because they are never useful
test: Which food is NOT an ingredient of granola? | Seeds | Nuts | Oats | *Cheese
test: Why might a hiker in Colorado eat a granola bar on a trail? | To read a map | To have a quick snack that is easy to carry | *To keep her shoes dry | To wash her hands
exam: Which sentence uses granola correctly? | I drank a granola with a spoon | I ate three granolas for breakfast | *I poured some granola into my bowl | I bought a granola of nuts and seeds
